    "q-340873": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340873",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [0,2) \\\\ -1, & x\\in [-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{3}.$\n\n${a}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2.$ Also, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,2)$ and $f(x)=-1$ for $x\\in (-2,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
